      "title": "The new rules of context engineering for Claude 5 generation models",
      "url": "https://claude.com/blog/the-new-rules-of-context-engineering-for-claude-5-generation-models",
      "discuss_url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49051361",
      "score": 448,
      "comments": 374,
      "author": "mellosouls",
      "published": "2026-07-25T20:42:35Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Structure prompts using explicit XML tags to organize complex multi-document inputs and prevent instruction drift in large context windows.",
        "Leverage Anthropic prompt caching on static system prompts and RAG contexts to dramatically cut latency and token costs.",
        "Position high-priority constraints and tool schemas near prompt boundaries to combat context degradation in multi-turn agent workflows."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/programming",
      "category": "engineering",
      "title": "A shell colon does nothing. Use it anyway",
      "url": "https://refp.se/articles/your-shell-and-the-magic-colon",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/eidh3u",
      "score": 98,
      "comments": 39,
      "author": "refp",
      "published": "2026-07-25T11:33:00Z",
      "bullets": [
        "The POSIX shell colon (`:`) is a builtin no-op returning exit status 0, providing syntactically required placeholders for loops and conditionals.",
        "It safely triggers variable parameter expansions (e.g., `${VAR:=default}`) for side-effects without attempting to execute the resulting string as a command.",
        "Redirection using `: > file` rapidly truncates files without spawning external binaries, while `: <<'EOF'` enables robust multi-line shell comments."
      ]
    },
    {
      "source": "lobsters",
      "subsource": "lobste.rs/security",
      "category": "security",
      "title": "Silent Replacement of Trusted macOS App Executables",
      "url": "https://mysk.blog/2026/07/23/macos-overwrite-app-executables/",
      "discuss_url": "https://lobste.rs/s/dxaogf",
      "score": 21,
      "comments": 3,
      "author": "juri",
      "published": "2026-07-23T13:37:15Z",
      "bullets": [
        "Attackers can silently replace Mach-O binaries within trusted macOS app bundles, inheriting existing TCC entitlements without triggering Gatekeeper alerts.",
        "Insecure file permissions on application bundles allow non-root processes to overwrite trusted executables without invalidating code signing requirements.",
        "Detection requires monitoring macOS Endpoint Security Framework (ESF) events for unexpected write or rename operations targeting Application binaries."
      ]
    },
